Z||000010||rockcrawl||12-03-2001||08:39 PM||||Yes, the Caddy uses a stupid linear actuator to control idle speed by pushing on the throttle linkage and opening the plates. They call it an ISC motor, it also contains a switch that opens when the accelerator is depressed to tell the ECM that you are opening the throttle. I haven't figured out a way to control this thing with a 730. I'm sure someone could come up with a circuit that converts the bi-polar stepper motor control to a voltage that will operate the motor, but it's not worth it. <P> On the car I am running now, I have replaced the ISC with a kicker solenoid from some old carbuerator. I turned on the second fan output of the 730 and set the temps to control it. When the engine is started cold, the solenoid is energized and opens the throttle to a preset position just slightly higher than the base idle. When the engine warms to the preset temp (~120) it de-energizes and falls back to base idle which is set by the idle adjusting screw. It's a primitive way to control idle speed, but it works very well. The ECM doesn't care that there is no IAC connected.<P> The other option is to get a throttle body from a TBI V8 that has a standard IAC. The bolt pattern is the same. You can remove the injectors for a cleaner install, although it is not neccesary to do so. You need about a 2" spacer (two 1" spacers?) under it so that the IAC motor will clear the EGR valve. The spacer is also required to seal off the IAC air channel on the bottom of the unit. Or you can remove and block off the EGR and deprogram it from the chip, and just put a thin plate under the tb. <P> A third option is a bit more involved, but possibly the best and neatest way to go. Pick a nice big GM throttle body of your choice and fabricate an adapter or plenum to make it fit. This is what I will be doing on the rest of my conversions (three more to go). I have been buying used throttle bodies on Ebay. The first was a 3" LS1 tb. I haven't had time to test it yet. I also have throttle bodies from a Sy/Ty, an L98 and an LT1 which are all basicly the same design. They will fit nicely over the distributor and an adapter is easy to make from two short pieces of 2" mandrel bent tubing and two plates.<P>here's a picture of the truck TBI, injectors removed, with no EGR. I welded a nipple on the vacuum side of the EGR block off plate to be used for power brake booster because the TBI didn't have one. This setup worked well, but I took it off because the bores were quite a bit smaller than they should be.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/trucktbi.JPG">  <P>And here's a pic of the LS1 tb with a plenum that I made from 3" and 2" steel pipe. Notice how the EGR fits. It sticks way up, but the notchback hood still closes.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ls1tb.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cooter||12-03-2001||10:09 PM||||Thanks Rockcrawl, that is what I needed to know. Now. . .<BR>1.) Which auto tranny are you using?<BR>2.) Does the distributer wiring match up to the '730 (like pulse hi/low, est and stuff like that)?<P>Thanks again-<BR>Greg||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||12-03-2001||10:40 PM||||I'm using a 4T60 from an '87 Olds 3.8. You can't use a 4T60E with the 730. The Caddy distributor is the same as a SBC HEI, but with a different base and shaft. It's a perfect match for the ECM, wire colors and circuit names are the same. In fact, most of the wire colors on the Caddy engine are the same as an F-body. You can use three of the ISC wires for IAC and add the fourth, or use the cruise control wires if you won't be having CC. The '90 Caddy harness also has extra wires for A.I.R. select and divert that won't be used, and power steering pressure. The eight injector wires need to be grouped into two sets of four, 1-3-5-7, and 2-4-6-8. You need to add a knock sensor and one wire for it. There's a spot at the rear of the block up on top like where Ford puts them. You need to drill and tap a hole in the aluminum, there's nothing under it except the empty space where the flywheel is. I could not find any other place to put a knock sensor. The easiest way to do the ECM conversion is to get an extra set of ECM plugs. Locate each circuit one by one on the Caddy harness and swap the wires over to the new plugs in the correct position for the 730. You'll find about half of the wires get put back in the same position they came from. If you just pull them out all at once, you'll end up tracing each wire back to the engine to figure out what it is. <P> I started with a stock 305 TPI prom, but it's actually pretty far off. I still need to do some fine tuning.<P>Jon||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cooter||12-03-2001||11:06 PM||||Thanks again- you have already saved us several headaches! The ecm wiring is not a problem, we have done those projects before (TPI 350/700 r4 in a '48 Ford), but this one is just more different than I was expecting.<BR>Did you make your own mounts or did you buy a pattern somewhere?<BR>On the PROM, is the stock fueling too lean or rich? I have Terry's software to edit the bin with, would you mind sending my what you have so maybe I'd have a starting point?<P>Greg||||reg||1
Z||000014||PBJ||12-04-2001||01:40 PM||||Have you had any problems running without and egr valve? ie. ping/detonation <P>Thanks<P>Pete||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||12-04-2001||05:46 PM||||I always make my own mounts. The L98 prom was rich on some cells and lean in others. The spark and fueling need to be adjusted over the entire range. I'll send you the binary that I'm currently using. You need to run premium fuel or it will ping like crazy. I had been tuning the car on 93, and acidently put 87 in her.... ended up dumping in a bottle of octane boost. It still needs work, I just don't have the time right now. Wish I had a chassis dyno, that would make it easier, happier neighbors too!. I started tuning without the EGR hooked up. I later hooked it up to reduce pinging, which I guess it did. I probably gained a few degrees of advance. The EGR robs power big-time, but possibly due to other tuning problems. The stock F-body setting was at 100% duty cycle, I trimmed it back to 20% and it's pretty much un-noticeable. At 100% there was a very noticeable decrease in performance. So anyway, if you choose to run without EGR, you just need to cut back on the advance a bit. With the knock sensor and a scantool, you can advance it right to the the point where you get detonation, and then cut back a few degrees.||||reg||1
Z||000016||PBJ||12-04-2001||09:44 PM||||About PROMs, I am alittle un-educated in this area (I know what they do, just nothing about making a new one up and knowing what to change). Is the 5.0L TPI F body PROM the way to go until you have your "tailored" PROM available.<P>Pete||||reg||1
